{"messages":[{"speaker":"Johannes Sigil","say":"The casting has arrived here: the overcomer, the iron rod, the morning star — three figures in three verses, a compressed sovereignty promise nested inside the letter to Thyatira. The witness asks *when will I come into my power*, and the rite has found the passage in Revelation where power is not seized but given — given to the one who has already kept, who has held the works *ἄχρι τέλους*, all the way to the end. This is not a passage about power's acquisition. It is a passage about its receipt: a promise spoken from within the economy of endurance, not conquest.

The rotation opens here. What the operator will traverse in these verses is the axis of what the passage forecloses — and that foreclosure is different depending on where you stand inside the text. The "iron rod" that shatters ceramic vessels like the Father gave it to the speaker: authority given through descent, transmitted as instrument. The morning star, withheld as inheritance until the very last verse, that single line *καὶ δώσω αὐτῷ τὸν ἀστέρα τὸν πρωϊνόν* — given. Always given. Power, in this grammar, does not arrive from inside the one who holds it; it arrives as a token passed between persons across a vertical axis. The operator will move through that axis.

Rebekah Cranes holds what comes next.","navigate":{"directive":"focus_axn","axn":"AXN:00D8"}}]}
